<!doctype html>
<!--
  The gscore landing page.

  Built by web/build.sh, which engraves the scores with gscore itself and
  drops them into the placeholders below. What you see is the program's own
  output, not a picture of it.

  Keep the placeholder names out of the prose here: they are substituted
  everywhere they appear, comments included, and a comment that named one
  had a copy of every score pasted into it.

  (C) Copyright 2026 Sebastien Tricaud. MIT licence; see LICENSE.
-->
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>gscore — music you can send to anyone</title>
<meta name="description" content="A free, MIT-licensed music score editor. Write music, hear it, and share it as a link that holds the notes themselves — no account, no upload, no server.">
<link rel="icon" href="icon.png">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="site.css">
</head>
<body>

<header class="top">
  <a class="brand" href="./"><img src="gilbert.svg" alt="" width="28" height="28"> gscore</a>
  <nav>
    <a href="scores/">Scores</a>
    <a href="docs/">Docs</a>
    <a href="https://github.com/stricaud/gscore">Source</a>
    <a class="cta" href="app/">Open the editor</a>
  </nav>
</header>

<main>

<section class="hero">
  <h1>Music you can send to&nbsp;anyone.</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    No account. No upload. No server at all — a gscore link <em>is</em> the
    music. Press play, change it, then copy the link and paste it to someone.
  </p>

  <div class="player">
    <div id="pieces" class="tabs" role="group" aria-label="Choose a piece">@TABS@</div>
    <div class="share">
      <button id="play" class="play">Play it</button>
      <button id="copy">Copy the link</button>
      <a id="open" class="button" href="app/">Open it in the editor</a>
      <a id="file" class="button" href="scores/" download>Download the page</a>
      <p id="said" role="status"></p>
      <p class="fine">
        Pressing play loads gscore itself into this page — the same program as
        the desktop build, compiled to WebAssembly, with its own cursor
        travelling the music. The link carries the notes themselves —
        <span id="size"></span> after the <code>#</code>, which browsers never
        send to a server. Nothing is stored anywhere and nothing expires.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="stage" id="stage">
      <p class="by"><span id="by"></span></p>
      @PIECES@
      <p class="note" id="note"></p>
      <!-- Until somebody asks, this is a picture of the piece and nothing
           is downloaded. Pressing play puts the real editor in here. -->
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="points">
  <article>
    <h2>Free, and yours</h2>
    <p>
      MIT licensed, all of it. No subscription, no tier that unlocks playback,
      no watermark on the parts you print. Your scores are files on your
      machine in a format that is written down, and a plain-text one at that.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h2>It opens for the other person</h2>
    <p>
      Send a link and they get the music, engraved and playable, in whatever
      browser they already have — no install, no sign-up, nothing to agree to.
      Or send one self-contained HTML file that works with no internet at all,
      for as long as they keep it.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h2>The same program everywhere</h2>
    <p>
      macOS, Windows and Linux run the same engraver as this page does — the
      core is compiled to WebAssembly here and to a native binary there. The
      music you are looking at was engraved by it.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

<section class="formats">
  <h2>One piece, every way out of it</h2>
  <p>
    Sharing is not only a link. gscore writes what the person at the other end
    can actually open, whatever they use:
  </p>
  <ul class="grid">
    <li><b>PDF</b><span>to print, or to email a part</span></li>
    <li><b>MusicXML</b><span>for Sibelius, Dorico, MuseScore</span></li>
    <li><b>LilyPond</b><span>for anyone who sets music by hand</span></li>
    <li><b>MIDI</b><span>for a sequencer or a keyboard</span></li>
    <li><b>FLAC</b><span>an actual recording, through real instruments</span></li>
    <li><b>SVG &amp; HTML</b><span>for a web page, like this one</span></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    And it <em>reads</em> ABC, MusicXML, MuseScore and NoteWorthy Composer
    files — including old ones, with their mistakes logged rather than
    refused. <a href="scores/">See the sample scores</a> for every format of
    each piece.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="get">
  <h2>Get it</h2>
  <p>Version @VERSION@. Each download plays real instruments the first time it opens — the soundfont is inside.</p>
  <div class="downloads">
    <a class="button" href="https://github.com/stricaud/gscore/releases/latest">macOS <small>.dmg</small></a>
    <a class="button" href="https://github.com/stricaud/gscore/releases/latest">Windows <small>installer</small></a>
    <a class="button" href="https://github.com/stricaud/gscore/releases/latest">Linux <small>.tar.gz / .deb</small></a>
    <a class="button ghost" href="app/">or just use it here</a>
  </div>
  <p class="fine">
    The macOS and Windows builds are signed by nobody, because a certificate is
    an annual fee and this is a free program; the release notes say which button
    to press the first time.
  </p>
</section>

</main>

<footer>
  <p>
    gscore @VERSION@ — written in C and GTK+ from 2001 to 2005, rewritten in Go
    for 2026. MIT licensed.
    <a href="https://github.com/stricaud/gscore">Source</a> ·
    <a href="docs/">Documentation</a> ·
    <a href="scores/">Scores</a>
  </p>
  <p class="fine">
    Music set in Bravura (SIL Open Font License). The sample pieces are public
    domain.
  </p>
</footer>

<script src="share.js"></script>
<script src="hero.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
